{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES REQUESTED on exact clean pushed head f61340c01c157bf82cbddf3792fa3075e924d362.\n\nArchitecture: this remains layer 1, Ordinary Git storage, through internal/gitstore. The exact head preserves the documented adapter contract and introduces no higher-layer meaning. The 1 MiB railway commit bound is a reasonable implementation bound for a prose row and does not change the architecture contract, so no architecture-page update is required.\n\nSecurity: the shared AuditBatch reuse now correctly provides exact header syntax, bounded allocation, OID/type/size/terminator checks, object-hash recomputation, poisoning, and exact-payload NUL scanning. Focused tests and vet pass. One prior condition is still open: a surplus complete response after the last requested object is accepted. parseCatFileHeader rejects surplus fields inside one header; that is not the same case. AuditBatch.Close closes stdin and calls Wait without reading stdout, while refuseMalformedCommits defers Close and discards its error. Thus final surplus bytes are never observed, no test covers them, and the earlier ratified requirement for surplus responses was narrowed to surplus fields without the requester agreeing.\n\nSimplification: replacing runExact/checkBatchResponses with AuditBatch is the correct simplification and removes the duplicated weaker protocol. Only a small completion remains. Make Close boundedly prove stdout EOF after closing stdin, propagate a successful-path Close error from Graph, and add a load-bearing surplus-complete-response test. Also rename TestGraphRefusesAnObjectThatIsNotTheOneNamed to what it exercises: it calls AuditBatch.readObject directly and tests an absent object, not Graph or hash mismatch. Then publish a fresh exact-head artifact and review request.\n\nThe corrected Git-versus-argv attribution, exact header tests, object bound, and shared hash verification are otherwise acceptable.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c5